226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,81,62,16,4779,400,81,62,17,4779,57960,67816,4779,400,81,15159,24,23,31716,387,279,19703,315,198,78045,87,47822,24,23,92,488,856,47822,24,22,92,488,856,47822,24,21,92,488,1124,67816,488,856,61,17,488,856,488,220,16,284,220,15,7110,60,9885,198,59,26056,37018,90,81,62,16,61,17,15170,81,62,16,488,220,16,92,488,1124,37018,90,81,62,17,61,17,15170,81,62,17,488,220,16,92,488,1124,67816,488,1124,37018,90,81,15159,24,23,92,61,17,15170,81,15159,24,23,92,488,220,16,92,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $r_1,$ $r_2,$ $\\dots,$ $r_{98}$ be the roots of\n\\[x^{98} + x^{97} + x^{96} + \\dots + x^2 + x + 1 = 0.\\]Find\n\\[\\frac{r_1^2}{r_1 + 1} + \\frac{r_2^2}{r_2 + 1} + \\dots + \\frac{r_{98}^2}{r_{98} + 1}.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"4a798512a6002ac0f54c431dff14185f63a9537562e818a5fc23f19aec39731d"},"seconds":62.01535892486572,"seed":150249890916616917,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we start by recognizing that the roots of the polynomial \\(x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x^2 + x + 1 = 0\\) are the 98th roots of unity, excluding 1. These roots are given by \\(r_k = e^{2\\pi i k / 99}\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, \\dots, 98\\).\n\nWe are asked to find the sum \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}\\). To simplify this expression, we first note that for each root \\(r_k\\) of the polynomial \\(x^{99} - 1 = 0\\) (excluding 1), we have \\(r_k^{99} = 1\\). Therefore, \\(r_k^{99} - 1 = (r_k - 1)(r_k^{98} + r_k^{97} + \\dots + r_k + 1) = 0\\). Since \\(r_k \\neq 1\\), it follows that \\(r_k^{98} + r_k^{97} + \\dots + r_k + 1 = 0\\).\n\nWe need to simplify \\(\\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1}\\). Notice that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k^2 + r_k - r_k}{r_k + 1} = \\frac{r_k(r_k + 1) - r_k}{r_k + 1} = r_k - \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum we need to find is:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\left( r_k - \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1} \\right) = \\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k - \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the 98th roots of unity, excluding 1, is \\(-1\\) (since the sum of all 99th roots of unity is 0). Therefore, \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} r_k = -1\\).\n\nNow we need to find \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1}\\). Consider the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^{98} + x^{97} + \\dots + x + 1\\). The roots of \\(P(x)\\) are \\(r_k\\), and we can use the identity \\(r_k^{99} = 1\\) to simplify \\(\\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1}\\). Notice that:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1} = 1 - \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1} = \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} \\right) = 98 - \\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}.\n\\]\n\nThe sum \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1}\\) can be found by considering the polynomial \\(Q(x) = P(x-1)\\), whose roots are \\(r_k + 1\\). The sum of the reciprocals of the roots of \\(Q(x)\\) is the negative of the coefficient of \\(x\\) divided by the constant term. After some algebraic manipulation, it can be shown that \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{1}{r_k + 1} = 49\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k}{r_k + 1} = 98 - 49 = 49.\n\\]\n\nFinally, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{98} \\frac{r_k^2}{r_k + 1} = -1 - 49 = -50.\n\\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-50}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-50}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
